  "query_preview": "../ Tuesday through Sunday/ Upper level pattern is \nrather dynamic through the long range. It starts out with a broad \nridge of high pressure situated over the Gulf of Alaska while a \nsharp trough is at around 180 longitude. That trough will be \nadvancing east through the start of the long range period and will\nproceed to push the ridge east into Canada by Thursday. Behind it\nmore zonal flow takes over, but not for long. An upper level low \nwill be digging SE from Russia by the weekend and will eventually \nanchor a broad trough stretching from the Bering Sea to the \nPanhandle by Sunday.\n\nThis means that the forecast for the panhandle is rather dynamic \nas well. It will start out rather quiet with the ridge over the \ngulf initially keeping most precipitation at bay. However, that \nridge will be starting to flatten enough that flow turns more \nonshore over the northern panhandle and precipitation starts to \nreturn to the forecast. The rain is not expected to start in \nernest until Wednesday as a front works its way eastward across \nthe gulf. This front does have some tropical connections, but it \nis moving eastward at a decent clip and looks like it will be \nweakening as it goes. So while there may be periods of moderate or\nheavy rain rates (especially for the NE Gulf coast), the rapid \neastward movement and weakening nature means that those rain rates \nwill not be over any particular area for long. Expected rain \ntotals for Wed look to be around 1 to 3 inches mainly from the \ncentral panhandle Northwestward with the highest totals expected \nalong the NE gulf coast. This system will also bring some wind \nwith 25 to 30 kt SW winds possible in the gulf while the northern \ninner channels will see a burst of southerly winds up to 30 kt \nduring the day Wednesday.\n\nThe general trend for late week and beyond is first a drying trend\nfor late week as surface ridging builds over the panhandle through\nlingering onshore flow could mean that showers and clouds will be\nstill around. The latter half of next weekend is looking to be\nrather active as a more substantial front is possible as the\nstrong upper low that is moving into Bering sea sends a front\nacross the gulf. We will be watching this feature as some guidance\nis suggesting this front could be min gale force as it crosses the\ncentral gulf and somewhat wet as well.",
